xnu 0345: the panel canvas is double buffered

The compositor's frame is copied into the same 18 MB the interface is fetching:
the copy costs 2.9-3.3 ms and the fetch about as much, and on most ticks the two
overlap, so the frame the panel receives is part old and part new -- the band
that walks down the glass while the UI moves (measured 2026-11-17: `copy avg
2916-3344 us`, `late avg 6499-11142 us`, 47-57 fps).

`mondrianfb_dbuf=1` splits the panel carve-out's first frame in two: the pipes
name the bootloader's buffer at 0xb8000000, the copy fills the next 18 MB
(0xb9194000) while that one is on the wire, and the tick points every pipe that
named the old address at the new one before the CTL flush and start. The
brightness DMA page at 0xbaaf0000 sits above both and is not touched. Off by
default; the host's panel capture and the driver's own diagnostics read the
buffer that is on the wire.

The second canvas is mapped write combining through an IOMemoryDescriptor
(`createMappingInTask` with kIOMapWriteCombineCache), not with `ml_io_map` on
the whole range: that allocates the 18 MB in one piece and fails at the first
kick, which takes the timer tick with it (measured 2026-11-17: `kick on` was the
last line of the tick, no frame and no per-second statistic ever reached the
ring, and the panel kept the kernel's text). A descriptor mapping reserves the
range and fills it in as it is written, and when it fails the driver says so and
stays single buffered.

